Z||000000||Pheagey||12-23-2001||07:31 PM||||   Ok, when driving striaght My steering wheel is slighte turned to the left. If I hold the wheel perfectly level I go to the left. Any ideas?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Devotshka||12-23-2001||07:34 PM||||you need to have your wheel locked straight when you get your alignment done   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <BR>If your car pulls to one side, one wheel is out of alignment, if it goes straight but the steering wheel is off, then the wheels are in alignment with each other, but not with the car.... if that made any sense...<P>&lt;edit&gt;Although now that i think of it, if you have a stock steering wheel, i *believe* you can just pull it off and push it back on in the right position...i'm pretty sure anyway.  Aftermarket wheels no such luck though.&lt;/edit&gt;<p>[This message has been edited by Devotshka (edited 12-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||gggg-us||12-23-2001||07:55 PM||||Along these lines... I just purchased my first fiero a few weeks ago, and when I get into it, my knees hit the steering wheel and it's kind of hard to get into. Once I'm in, it's fine, just getting in is hard...I feel like an old man saying it's hard to get into my car...anyway. The car doesn't have a tilt steering wheel...did they make any with it? If not, then what can be done to remedy this? I did see a picture of a car with a steering wheel that wasn't fully round, it had a flat portion on the bottom of the wheel. I assume maybe a smaller wheel would make it easier, but they you'll have to pull harder on the wheel to get in to turn, on a car that already doesn't have power steering.<BR>Can the column be angled up just a little to provide a little more room? Just and inch or two would improve the situation greatly.<P>------------------<BR>88 4Cyl ... Rebuilding 81 Vette Engine for it now.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||12-23-2001||08:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gggg-us:<BR><B>Along these lines... I just purchased my first fiero a few weeks ago, </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>First, let me say congratulations!  I hope you enjoy your Fiero.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>and when I get into it, my knees hit the steering wheel and it's kind of hard to get into.......The car doesn't have a tilt steering wheel...did they make any with it? If not, then what can be done to remedy this? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The vast majority of Fieros came with a tilt column.......you were just "lucky" enough to get a base coupe without it.  I imagine you have no other optionas as well (no sunroof, etc.).  <P>You can swap in a tilt column from another Fiero.  It would be a simple bolt on upgrade.......you just need to find one and put it on.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||GTDude||12-23-2001||08:05 PM||||If the steering pulls to one side when you take your hands off of the wheel....check the tires first.  I am a GM tech working for a GoodYear store......I see cars pulling due to broken belts in tires all day long....check this closely if it pulls with hands off wheel.  Good luck!<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||jelly2m8||12-23-2001||08:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfiero:<BR><B> The vast majority of Fieros came with a tilt column.......you were just "lucky" enough to get a base coupe without it.  I imagine you have no other optionas as well (no sunroof, etc.).  <P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> Nifty, that makes 2 that I know of so far.<BR>My neighbors 86 plain jane 5 spd coupe does not have the tilt steering column either.<P> Wonder what the production numbers are on non-tilt? They gotta be rare.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bill Shenefelt||12-23-2001||08:49 PM||||To avoid $60 or so for another alignment, just adjust both tie rod ends the same number of turns.  Start with 2 full turns and see what that does.  Turn the steering wheel straight and loosen one tie rod end and tighten the other.  If the car goes left with wheel straight, Adjust the the drivers side to pull the left wheel in at the front. and adjust the passengers side to push the right wheel out on the front..  Watch the number of turns and be sure to do the SAME number on both sides.  Don't bother with moving the steering wheel on the spline.  You will probably miss the right point anyway trying that.<BR>Bill Shenefelt||||reg||1
Z||000007||jelly2m8||12-23-2001||09:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Bill Shenefelt:<BR><B>To avoid $60 or so for another alignment, just adjust both tie rod ends the same number of turns.  Start with 2 full turns and see what that does.  Turn the steering wheel straight and loosen one tie rod end and tighten the other.  If the car goes left with wheel straight, Adjust the the drivers side to pull the left wheel in at the front. and adjust the passengers side to push the right wheel out on the front..  Watch the number of turns and be sure to do the SAME number on both sides.  Don't bother with moving the steering wheel on the spline.  You will probably miss the right point anyway trying that.<BR>Bill Shenefelt</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> Sorry Bill, don't recommend that, you can't simply make 2 turns or what ever on each side and expect the toe-in to stay correct. Alignment machines cost around 25 thousand dollars, alignment shops just don't fork that money over if a tape measure and a few turns here, and a few turns there would do.<P> Dude, spend the 60 bux or what ever and have all 4 wheels aligned. Obviously a part was replaced on the car, or the alignment was not done correctly in the first place.<P> Not only will you car handle properly, and you have a straight steering wheel, but your tires will last longer. This alone will offset the charge for the alignment.<P> If you don't have the cash, try moving the steering wheel as Devotshka suggested. <P> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||12-24-2001||07:25 AM||||How do you think the service guys set it jelly. You turn the tie rod ends. If you move them both same number of turns, you wont change any alignment. I do perfect wheel alignment on my own cars with a piece of string (toe) and a surveyors bubble level (camber) and road test trial and error (caster). Did you ever see an alignment rack at a racetrack........||||reg||1
Z||000009||jelly2m8||12-24-2001||08:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>How do you think the service guys set it jelly. You turn the tie rod ends. If you move them both same number of turns, you wont change any alignment. I do perfect wheel alignment on my own cars with a piece of string (toe) and a surveyors bubble level (camber) and road test trial and error (caster). Did you ever see an alignment rack at a racetrack........</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR> Thats great that you are having good luck Roger.<P> Decent service guys will get it close when they install parts, but they will also recommend getting an alignment as soon as possible.<P> I am a certified alignment technician with 12 years experiance among my other trades.<BR> I do know correct alignments inside, outside, and upside down.<P> Maybe thats why alot of people that ride in my cars wonder why my cars handle better than theirs, a proper alignment perhaps?<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||12-24-2001||01:06 PM||||You may be one of the very few that takes a little pride in setting proper alignment. Most only care about getting it close with the least amount of time spent. Ive never know one to align a Fiero around here and changing the caster by reshimming the a arm-too much bother. Every alignment Ive had done was not correct, close maybe but I had to tweak it myself to get it right. I even remember taking a Mustang in once, was told that there are no adjustments that could be done.... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"> They just want to save time for doing simpler jobs that make more money.||||reg||1
